{:edb/db-before-t 1220 :edb/db-after-t 1221 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534533 50 #inst "2026-10-12T02:14:11.714Z" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186168687 1078 #edb/ref 17592186168689 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186168693 1078 #edb/ref 17592186168696 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186168705 1078 #edb/ref 17592186168706 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168712 13194139534533 17592186045417 false] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168713 13194139534533 17592186045417 false] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168714 13194139534533 17592186045417 false] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168715 13194139534533 17592186045417 false] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186168716 13194139534533 17592186045417 false] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424462 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424477 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424483 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424484 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186168711 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424496 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186168711 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424477 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424515 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424516 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172099 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424520 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172100 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424519 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172100 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424521 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172100 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424522 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172100 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424524 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172101 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424514 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172101 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424517 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172101 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424518 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172101 1038 #edb/ref 17592186424523 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172181 1050 600.0 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172304 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172304 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨3{x}^{5}+2y,\\,3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0,0),(1,1),$ and $(-1,1).$" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172304 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$F(x, y) = ⟨3x^{5} + 2y, 3y^{2} - e^{x}⟩$\nthrough the boundary of the triangle with vertices $(0, 0), (1, 1)$, and $(-1, 1)$." 13194139534533 17592186045417 false] [17592186172304 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/26/269e3c7f92cee66c78f3226e7c967eb51153503c598d2e4d1e4b56a33e646ac4.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3{x}^{5}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)=3{y}^{2}-{e}^{x}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=15{x}^{4},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =6y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-y\\le x\\le y,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(15{x}^{4}+6y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-y}^{y}(15{x}^{4}+6y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[3{x}^{5}+6xy]_{-y}^{y}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(3{y}^{5}+6{y}^{2})-(-3{y}^{5}-6{y}^{2})]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6{y}^{5}+12{y}^{2})\\,dy \\\\  & =[{y}^{6}+4{y}^{3}]_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(1+4)-0 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172305 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172305 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+2y,\\,{x}^{2}+2xy⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y={x}^{2}$ and the lines $y=0$ and $x=1.$" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172305 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$F(x, y) = ⟨x^{2} + 2y, x^{2} + 2xy⟩$\nthrough the boundary of the region enclosed by the curve $y = x^{2}$ and the lines $y = 0$ and $x = 1$." 13194139534533 17592186045417 false] [17592186172305 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/91/9168d2ed7eeaa5f1dd331a0a986ac7ecfd6b964217a2cb614deb0cdd54437d65.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+2y$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{2}+2xy.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the region $D$ as a type I region as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le {x}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{{x}^{2}}4x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4xy\\,{|}_{0}^{{x}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,dx \\\\  & ={x}^{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & ={1}^{4}-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172307 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172307 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1.$" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172307 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n$F(x, y) = ⟨\\cos (πy) - x^{2}, \\sin (πx) + 2y⟩$\nthrough the boundary of the square $0 \\le x \\le 1, 0 \\le y \\le 1$." 13194139534533 17592186045417 false] [17592186172307 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/37/3786d0a5fb99d1a32d761e067f9c49b6b7da05a11fc555dd94a4ef42d753f129.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi y)-{x}^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi x)+2y.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=-2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 1\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2x+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-2x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[2x-{x}^{2}]_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}[(2-1)-0]\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,dy \\\\  & =y\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172308 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172308 1071 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field\n$F(x, y) = ⟨x^{2} + 1, y^{2}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0, 0), (1, 0)$, and $(0, 2)$ can be represented as\n\n$∮_(C)F \\cdot nds = ∬_(D)f(x, y)dA$,\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C$, and $D$ is the region bounded by $C$. Calculate the function $f(x, y)$." 13194139534533 17592186045417 false] [17592186172308 1071 "According to the flux form of Green's theorem, the flux of the vector field \n$F(x,y)=⟨{x}^{2}+1,\\,{y}^{2}⟩$\nacross the triangle $C$ with vertices $(0,0),(1,0),$ and $(0,2)$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is traversed counterclockwise, $n$ is the outward-pointing unit normal vector to $C,$ and $D$ is the region bounded by $C.$ Calculate the function $f(x,y).$" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186172308 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n \n\n![](images/c3/c3c617522488e6ebf47dd2b2a52c43a1fe06ccaef8cd1e8752ab4df5e2d4bd64.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}+1$ and $Q(x,y)={y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the flux form of Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2x+2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2(x+y)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y)=2(x+y).$" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186173207 1064 "There is some nice intuition behind the flux form of Green's theorem that makes it easier to remember.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P$ and $Q$ defined over a region $D.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf F$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D.$\n\n![](images/47/47a81125069be94b4ccda6f228ff1b8253f99595c6f3c4ae0ca710bdb796215f.png)\n\n    \n\nRecall that the divergence (i.e., the microscopic flux) of $\\mathbf F$ at a point is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the divergence at every point over the entire domain $D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us one side of Green's theorem in flux form.\n\nThe macroscopic divergence is simply the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the divergences with the flux, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A = \\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s.\n$$\n\nThe flux form of Green's theorem can be summarized in a single picture, as follows:\n\n![](images/0f/0f8ba4b7e853e3b59e8677b0954c3cfae1b9c482750e13a742bd4d45eda8005d.png)" 13194139534533 17592186045417 true] [17592186173207 1064 "There is some nice intuition behind the flux form of Green's theorem that makes it easier to remember.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf F$ is a vector field on $\\mathbb R^2$ with component functions $P$ and $Q$ defined over a region $D.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\mathbf F(x,y) = P(x,y)\\,\\mathbf i + Q(x,y)\\,\\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem in flux form states that the sum of the microscopic \"fluxes\" (divergences) of $\\mathbf F$ inside $D$ equals the macroscopic flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D.$\n\n![](images/47/47a81125069be94b4ccda6f228ff1b8253f99595c6f3c4ae0ca710bdb796215f.png)\n\nRecall that the divergence (i.e., the microscopic flux) of $\\mathbf F$ at a point is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm{div}\\,\\mathbf F = \\nabla \\cdot \\mathbf F = \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y}.\n$$\n\nIf we sum (integrate) the divergence at every point over the entire domain $D,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial P }{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial Q }{\\partial y} \\right)\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThis expression gives us one side of Green's theorem in flux form.\n\nThe macroscopic divergence is simply the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across the boundary of $D{:}$\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_C\\mathbf F\\cdot \\mathbf n \\, \\textrm d s\n$$\n\nEquating the sum of the divergences with the flux, we have\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D \\left(